WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:09.090
- So this will be a very different talk

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:10.140
than the last one we heard.

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:11.880
So like a lot of philosophers,

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:13.980
I'm interested in very general questions

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:15.600
and this is a question

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:18.517
that I've pursued in very different ways,

00:00:18.517 --> 00:00:20.190
"What are the nature, scope and limits

00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:21.930
of knowledge in physics?"

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:23.670
And other philosophers
of science have looked

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:24.750
at other fields of course.

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:26.670
I've just by chance ended up

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:29.610
and by interest, working
primarily on physics.

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:31.140
And really, I've been looking a lot

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:33.900
at trying to understand
what's going on in physics

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:36.180
in order to answer these sorts
of epistemological questions.

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:38.670
I've often pursued
historical case studies,

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:40.713
looking at the detailed development-

00:00:40.713 --> 00:00:41.546
- [Audience Member 1] Microphone please.

00:00:41.546 --> 00:00:42.379
- Oh, sorry,

00:00:42.379 --> 00:00:44.880
looking at the detailed
development of particular fields

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:47.880
as a way to try to address this question.

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:50.700
But today, I wanna talk
about a different question,

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:53.460
focusing on a type of sciences,

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:56.430
or a kind of approach
to scientific projects

00:00:56.430 --> 00:00:59.040
that I'll loosely call
historical sciences.

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:00.420
And so this, actually,

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:03.810
I should credit a former PhD
student of mine, Craig Fox,

00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:06.570
who worked on this for a
thesis a few years ago.

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:08.310
So it was really working with him

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:10.170
that got me interested in these questions.

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:12.630
And also, of course, it's
relevant to cosmology,

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:15.240
which is something I've
thought a lot about.

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:17.640
And it turns out that there's
a small group of people

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:19.080
within philosophy of science,

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:21.750
who've been looking at
this question as well.

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:25.470
So I think a shared theme

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:29.067
of discussions of
historical sciences is that,

00:01:29.067 --> 00:01:30.360
and this is not surprising

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:33.030
that a lot of the reasoning
in historical sciences,

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:35.520
is reconstructing history based on traces

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:37.350
that we have in the present.

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:39.450
And the question is how
much can we do with that?

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:43.200
So I think there was for a
while, a sort of crude assumption

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:45.120
that the historical
sciences were worse off,

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:47.220
compared to experimental sciences.

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:50.100
And that you'd face a kind
of under-determination threat

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:52.470
in historical sciences.

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:54.390
There are different ways
of expressing this worry.

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:56.490
One is just that you would
have a sort of decay,

00:01:56.490 --> 00:01:58.620
or loss of information over time,

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:01.170
so that the traces of
past events will be harder

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:02.730
to access later.

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:04.260
And also just the obvious fact

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:06.210
that we don't have the ability

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:08.030
to manipulate historical systems

00:02:08.030 --> 00:02:09.840
in the way that we often are able

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:12.540
to manipulate experimental systems.

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:13.920
So there's a variety of people,

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:15.990
who have classified as pessimists.

00:02:15.990 --> 00:02:17.100
Sober is more complicated.

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:18.510
He's optimistic about some things

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:20.943
and has some more pessimistic
arguments as well.

00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:23.640
Derek Turner had the misfortune

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:25.140
of doing what philosophers often do,

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:28.020
which is announce that certain
things will be impossible.

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:31.477
So he, as a side note in a paper, said,

00:02:31.477 --> 00:02:32.970
"Well, there are some
things we'll never know

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:33.803
about the past,

00:02:33.803 --> 00:02:36.094
such as the color of dinosaurs."

00:02:36.094 --> 00:02:36.927
(audience member laughing)

00:02:36.927 --> 00:02:38.850
Like a lot of philosophical
pronouncements of that sort,

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:41.430
that was like a prelude
to conquest by science.

00:02:41.430 --> 00:02:42.990
So a few years later,

00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:44.460
paleontologists gathered evidence

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:46.980
that seems to give them good grounds

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:49.473
for establishing what the
color of dinosaurs are.

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:52.680
So I think these are real concerns

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:55.530
and I think one of the
overall questions I often ask

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:57.000
in trying to address the big question,

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:59.880
is what are the evidential
challenges that a field faces?

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:02.310
And how have scientists
overcome those challenges

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:04.650
and to what extent have they done so?

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:07.230
The more recent literature
on historical sciences,

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:09.270
and here, Adrian Curry's
book is a good example,

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:11.550
you can see just in the
subtitle of the book,

00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:14.100
it's "An Optimist's Guide
to the Historical Sciences".

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:16.350
And so he's really defending the claim

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:19.440
that historical sciences have
been able to establish results

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:22.713
with as much certainty as we
have in other areas of science.

00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:25.680
And some of this work has focused

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:28.950
on case studies of specific
examples of historical sciences

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:32.970
that seem to have delivered
really firm results

00:03:32.970 --> 00:03:35.040
and knowledge of the past.

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:36.480
There's one kind of argument

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:40.350
that, Carol Cleland in particular
in a 2001 paper, argued

00:03:40.350 --> 00:03:43.680
that we can address the pessimist worry

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:46.500
by appealing to an asymmetry
of over-determination

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:48.720
that effects overdetermine causes.

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:50.340
And so there's a way
of thinking about this

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:52.470
that she's borrowing from
the Philosopher David Lewis

00:03:52.470 --> 00:03:56.730
and his analysis of the
asymmetry of causation.

00:03:56.730 --> 00:03:59.520
And I think although I'm much
more on the optimist side,

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:01.680
I find that way of framing the discussion

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:02.880
to be a bit limiting

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:05.160
and partly there's things
that I'll leave offstage,

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:08.310
just it's partly criticisms
of Lewis' analysis

00:04:08.310 --> 00:04:10.650
of counterfactual asymmetry.

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:11.970
But what I'd like to do today,

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:15.690
is to suggest a different
way of framing the discussion

00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:18.240
that's based on some of the causation

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:20.910
and especially the interventionist
approach to causation

00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:22.290
that I've been dipping into recently.

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:24.930
So this was an invitation
that came at a good time,

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:26.700
because I've just started looking at this

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:29.280
and so it's great to have benefited

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:30.630
from the talks yesterday.

00:04:30.630 --> 00:04:32.580
And you'll see that there's
a lot of common themes.

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:35.550
So the way I think that it's possible

00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:38.130
to pose the question slightly differently,

00:04:38.130 --> 00:04:40.830
is to say "What does the
world have to be like

00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:43.860
to enable reliable
reconstructions of the deep past,"

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:47.160
in so far as we have been able
to succeed in giving those.

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:49.290
And I'm gonna focus just on two aspects

00:04:49.290 --> 00:04:50.130
of this question here.

00:04:50.130 --> 00:04:53.700
So there's a phrase I think
is originally Jim Woodward's,

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:56.040
but it's in the title
of a paper by Naftali

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:59.400
and Jim and Porter Williams,
a really recent paper

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:00.780
that says,

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:02.880
the question is what's the
worldly infrastructure?

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:05.190
So what has to be present in the world

00:05:05.190 --> 00:05:08.730
to underwrite our ability
to make causal inferences?

00:05:08.730 --> 00:05:11.580
And so I think there's
some very similar features

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:14.390
that are important for
understanding causal inferences

00:05:14.390 --> 00:05:15.540
in the deep past.

00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:17.820
And so I should say also
I think it's important

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:19.200
that these are causal inferences.

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:20.310
Because I think the scientists,

00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:21.750
who are investigating the deep past,

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:24.810
are not just interested in
establishing what happened.

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:25.890
They're also interested

00:05:25.890 --> 00:05:28.230
in establishing causal
accounts of what happened.

00:05:28.230 --> 00:05:30.870
So I think it is crucial
that it's a causal inference

00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:33.273
and not just a reconstruction effort.

00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:35.610
And the other point I'll make,

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:42.440
so I think the methodology
of trace-based reasoning

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:44.610
and reconstructions based on traces,

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:46.590
is certainly substantially correct.

00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:49.470
But I think there's
actually a stronger notion.

00:05:49.470 --> 00:05:51.840
There are some cases, which
I'll call time capsules,

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:54.540
and it might be more a difference
of degree than of kind,

00:05:54.540 --> 00:05:55.950
but there are some kinds of systems

00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:59.010
that allow us to make
astonishingly good inferences

00:05:59.010 --> 00:06:00.150
about past states.

00:06:00.150 --> 00:06:03.120
And so I'm going to argue
that the feature of the world

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:05.130
that allows for those
kinds of time capsules,

00:06:05.130 --> 00:06:06.630
is a kind of decoupling

00:06:06.630 --> 00:06:09.870
that's been mentioned in a
few earlier talks already.

00:06:09.870 --> 00:06:11.910
So those are the two things
I'm going to focus on,

00:06:11.910 --> 00:06:14.250
the features that I think
the world has to have

00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:18.300
in order to underwrite
reconstructions of the deep past.

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:21.180
So let me start by just,
I'll first talk about this,

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:23.100
the relevant notion of independence

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:25.997
before going back to
decoupling and traces.

00:06:25.997 --> 00:06:27.390
And then I'll very briefly comment

00:06:27.390 --> 00:06:28.983
on early universe cosmology.

00:06:29.910 --> 00:06:33.660
So I'm gonna use an example here briefly

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:36.810
to illustrate the importance
of a notion of independence

00:06:36.810 --> 00:06:38.040
of the initial conditions.

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:40.620
So this goes back to the 19th century

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:45.480
when Ludwig Boltzmann derived
what he called the H-theorem.

00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:47.130
So the image across the top is meant

00:06:47.130 --> 00:06:50.370
to be something like an
ideal gas interacting

00:06:50.370 --> 00:06:52.710
where it's just paralyzed interactions

00:06:52.710 --> 00:06:57.510
between the gas molecules
bouncing off of each other.

00:06:57.510 --> 00:06:58.980
And this is meant to be a time series

00:06:58.980 --> 00:07:00.300
where you can see the,

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:01.500
and if you look at the red,

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:05.400
you can see, well, the red
dots move in such a way

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:06.233
that you could imagine

00:07:06.233 --> 00:07:09.150
that they've interacted
with the nearby particles

00:07:09.150 --> 00:07:11.250
and have bounced off of them.

00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:14.100
Now the striking thing
that Boltzmann derived,

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:17.010
is that he argued that
there's an evolution

00:07:17.010 --> 00:07:18.450
towards an equilibrium state,

00:07:18.450 --> 00:07:21.150
namely a particular distribution
of velocities in the gas,

00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:23.940
called the Maxwellian
Velocity Distribution.

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:26.790
But as various people soon pointed out,

00:07:26.790 --> 00:07:28.890
that was incredibly puzzling.

00:07:28.890 --> 00:07:31.410
Because the dynamics
describing these collisions,

00:07:31.410 --> 00:07:33.600
the pairwise collisions
between the molecules,

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:35.610
were completely time-reversal invariant.

00:07:35.610 --> 00:07:37.800
So how could you get a monotonic evolution

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:39.720
towards equilibrium

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:43.110
when you have time-reversal
invariant dynamics?

00:07:43.110 --> 00:07:46.410
And the source of the
asymmetry was an assumption

00:07:46.410 --> 00:07:48.030
that might've seemed innocent,

00:07:48.030 --> 00:07:50.100
which he called the (indistinct),

00:07:50.100 --> 00:07:52.440
which we usually call molecular chaos,

00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:55.260
which is that the interacting particles

00:07:55.260 --> 00:07:57.033
before they collide,

00:07:57.870 --> 00:08:01.470
their states, their velocities
and positions are independent

00:08:01.470 --> 00:08:02.303
of one another.

00:08:02.303 --> 00:08:03.136
They're not correlated.

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:06.690
But of course, that's a
time-asymmetric assumption.

00:08:06.690 --> 00:08:08.580
If you have the two particles collide,

00:08:08.580 --> 00:08:09.413
afterwards,

00:08:09.413 --> 00:08:11.310
their velocities and
positions will be correlated

00:08:11.310 --> 00:08:12.210
with one another.

00:08:12.210 --> 00:08:16.350
And so by initially setting
those correlations aside,

00:08:16.350 --> 00:08:18.000
you're introducing a subtle sort

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:20.703
of time-asymmetric initial condition.

00:08:25.260 --> 00:08:26.093
And you can think

00:08:26.093 --> 00:08:29.880
about how you could set up an
anti-thermodynamic evolution,

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:32.850
namely an ideal gas that
doesn't go towards equilibrium,

00:08:32.850 --> 00:08:36.030
doesn't go towards the
Maxwellian Velocity Distribution.

00:08:36.030 --> 00:08:38.880
So this is the way to set it up.

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:41.490
Consider any system that
evolves towards equilibrium.

00:08:41.490 --> 00:08:44.790
Now imagine that you can
reverse all of the velocities

00:08:44.790 --> 00:08:46.200
of the particles

00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:50.310
and the micro-state that
you've defined in this way

00:08:50.310 --> 00:08:51.600
under the dynamics,

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:53.520
there's an allowed evolution

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:56.250
that goes exactly the
opposite of the evolution

00:08:56.250 --> 00:08:58.470
that just got you into the state.

00:08:58.470 --> 00:09:02.070
And so that final micro-state
will then evolve away

00:09:02.070 --> 00:09:03.900
from equilibrium.

00:09:03.900 --> 00:09:06.330
But there's some really surprising
things about this, right?

00:09:06.330 --> 00:09:08.970
The first is that what you're doing,

00:09:08.970 --> 00:09:10.440
is taking all the correlations

00:09:10.440 --> 00:09:11.760
that exist in that final state

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:14.700
and you're using the dynamics
to sort of unwind those

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:17.283
to take you back towards
the initial state.

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:20.100
And so it's an
anti-thermodynamic evolution

00:09:20.100 --> 00:09:21.750
in the sense that this is not the kind

00:09:21.750 --> 00:09:24.270
of thing we see everyday gases doing,

00:09:24.270 --> 00:09:29.270
it's against all of the
features we see the evolution

00:09:29.490 --> 00:09:31.680
towards equilibrium.

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:32.790
But the thing I wanna focus on,

00:09:32.790 --> 00:09:35.880
is just that there are really two features

00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:39.060
of this anti-thermodynamic
evolution that we can think of

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:42.420
in terms of the independence of the state.

00:09:42.420 --> 00:09:43.830
So first of all,

00:09:43.830 --> 00:09:46.500
when I've defined the state this way,

00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:47.333
I've defined a state

00:09:47.333 --> 00:09:50.010
that has a huge number of
complicated correlations

00:09:50.010 --> 00:09:51.930
between the states of
the particles, right?

00:09:51.930 --> 00:09:53.880
That's how I've introduced it.

00:09:53.880 --> 00:09:56.250
And that's essential to have
those correlations present

00:09:56.250 --> 00:10:00.333
in order to be able to determine
that dynamical trajectory.

00:10:01.980 --> 00:10:04.380
There's a second point, which
is a little more subtle,

00:10:04.380 --> 00:10:08.160
is it's also the case that
the reverse state is dependent

00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:09.720
on the dynamics that I've used

00:10:09.720 --> 00:10:11.790
to describe the evolution of the gas.

00:10:11.790 --> 00:10:14.280
If I had slightly different dynamics,

00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:15.450
that abnormal state,

00:10:15.450 --> 00:10:17.190
even though it's got all the correlations,

00:10:17.190 --> 00:10:19.470
wouldn't evolve
anti-thermodynamically, right?

00:10:19.470 --> 00:10:24.180
So it's both, the state
has in the specification

00:10:24.180 --> 00:10:25.013
of the particles,

00:10:25.013 --> 00:10:27.600
they're all very delicately
dependent on each other.

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:29.670
And exactly what state it is that leads

00:10:29.670 --> 00:10:31.770
to the anti-thermodynamic evolution,

00:10:31.770 --> 00:10:33.570
depends on the knowledge of the dynamics

00:10:33.570 --> 00:10:34.720
of that system as well.

00:10:36.038 --> 00:10:39.210
And I should say, I have
various references here,

00:10:39.210 --> 00:10:42.090
very little of what I'm
saying now is original at all.

00:10:42.090 --> 00:10:43.980
It's just setting up a point,

00:10:43.980 --> 00:10:46.680
or maybe nothing I'm saying
at this point is original.

00:10:47.970 --> 00:10:49.230
Now I think there's a number

00:10:49.230 --> 00:10:52.890
of similar kinds of
assumptions about independence

00:10:52.890 --> 00:10:54.780
that arise in physics.

00:10:54.780 --> 00:10:57.090
So the first point is
we can just generalize

00:10:57.090 --> 00:10:58.470
what Boltzmann was talking about.

00:10:58.470 --> 00:11:01.500
And it arises in other cases

00:11:01.500 --> 00:11:05.100
where physicists in statistical mechanics,

00:11:05.100 --> 00:11:08.190
derive time-asymmetric
macroscopic dynamics,

00:11:08.190 --> 00:11:09.600
using coarse-graining and so on.

00:11:09.600 --> 00:11:11.670
I'm not going to go into
the details of that.

00:11:11.670 --> 00:11:15.150
But a lot of these technical practices,

00:11:15.150 --> 00:11:17.550
involve something much like what we saw

00:11:17.550 --> 00:11:18.510
in Boltzmann H-theorem,

00:11:18.510 --> 00:11:21.840
namely throwing away
correlations between subsystems

00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:26.553
in order to just to derive
an asymmetric evolution.

00:11:28.650 --> 00:11:30.930
You can also see this in an assumption

00:11:30.930 --> 00:11:32.760
of no source-free radiation.

00:11:32.760 --> 00:11:35.910
So this is often illustrated
using the example

00:11:35.910 --> 00:11:38.430
of a stone thrown into a pond.

00:11:38.430 --> 00:11:39.840
When we throw a stone in a pond,

00:11:39.840 --> 00:11:41.430
we see the waves ripple out.

00:11:41.430 --> 00:11:44.160
We very rarely see waves converge

00:11:44.160 --> 00:11:46.560
and cause a stone to be
thrown out of the pond.

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:49.560
That kind of evolution
would require the same sort

00:11:49.560 --> 00:11:52.920
of coherent evolution from the state

00:11:52.920 --> 00:11:55.290
in order to have the kind of time reversal

00:11:55.290 --> 00:11:57.930
of what we usually would expect to see.

00:11:57.930 --> 00:12:00.390
And so there's another
condition that you can impose

00:12:00.390 --> 00:12:03.243
that will rule out
exactly that kind of case.

00:12:04.080 --> 00:12:06.390
And finally, I mean we could go on,

00:12:06.390 --> 00:12:09.330
there's a lot of different cases
that illustrate this point,

00:12:09.330 --> 00:12:10.800
here's a more subtle one.

00:12:10.800 --> 00:12:11.633
It's actually quite,

00:12:11.633 --> 00:12:12.570
there's a recent paper

00:12:12.570 --> 00:12:14.400
by Williams, Miller and Dougherty

00:12:14.400 --> 00:12:17.340
that analyzes something
that Weinberg introduced

00:12:17.340 --> 00:12:19.350
as an axiom of QFT,

00:12:19.350 --> 00:12:22.050
the cluster decomposition
principle as he called it.

00:12:22.050 --> 00:12:25.170
So imagine you have clusters
of fields interacting

00:12:25.170 --> 00:12:26.130
with one another

00:12:26.130 --> 00:12:29.580
and then you take those to
be further and further away.

00:12:29.580 --> 00:12:32.490
And the claim is that the
vacuum expectation values,

00:12:32.490 --> 00:12:34.590
or the asymmetric elements
that you would use

00:12:34.590 --> 00:12:36.813
to calculate things in
particle accelerators,

00:12:36.813 --> 00:12:39.810
that those become independent
as you move those far away.

00:12:39.810 --> 00:12:41.490
So again, it's a way of saying

00:12:41.490 --> 00:12:44.190
that the probability
distribution factorizes

00:12:44.190 --> 00:12:47.400
as a precise way of making
this independence claim.

00:12:47.400 --> 00:12:48.930
And again, I think this
is the kind of thing

00:12:48.930 --> 00:12:51.960
that you will find in a lot of
different aspects of physics,

00:12:51.960 --> 00:12:53.860
independence assumptions of this sort.

00:12:54.870 --> 00:12:55.703
So I think,

00:12:55.703 --> 00:13:00.450
we can see the interventionist
approach to causation

00:13:00.450 --> 00:13:02.520
as Woodward formulates it

00:13:02.520 --> 00:13:05.670
as highlighting two very similar features

00:13:05.670 --> 00:13:08.010
to what I've just introduced
in terms of the physics.

00:13:08.010 --> 00:13:10.410
So these are more general statements

00:13:10.410 --> 00:13:13.020
of the same kind of
independence that I've seen,

00:13:13.020 --> 00:13:15.570
that I've been highlighting
as well-motivated physically.

00:13:15.570 --> 00:13:17.850
So this is, the first is very close

00:13:17.850 --> 00:13:19.260
to the Causal Markov Condition

00:13:19.260 --> 00:13:21.540
that we saw in Thomas' talk yesterday.

00:13:21.540 --> 00:13:24.270
I think this is actually
slightly weaker logically.

00:13:24.270 --> 00:13:26.760
But if we have a bunch
of random variables,

00:13:26.760 --> 00:13:28.530
if none are causes of other variables

00:13:28.530 --> 00:13:30.090
and don't share a common cause,

00:13:30.090 --> 00:13:32.220
then they should be
statistically independent.

00:13:32.220 --> 00:13:34.890
So that's like the first
claim I was saying.

00:13:34.890 --> 00:13:38.460
And the second, I don't
really like the name,

00:13:38.460 --> 00:13:39.810
but I guess it's good enough,

00:13:39.810 --> 00:13:41.400
value relationship independence.

00:13:41.400 --> 00:13:43.320
This is related to the idea

00:13:43.320 --> 00:13:46.860
that the way that the initial
variables are specified,

00:13:46.860 --> 00:13:48.720
should be independent of the law,

00:13:48.720 --> 00:13:51.390
or the causal generalization
that's relevant to them.

00:13:51.390 --> 00:13:54.690
So these are exactly the
two features we saw earlier

00:13:54.690 --> 00:13:57.333
in the case of the Boltzmann H-theorem.

00:14:02.351 --> 00:14:05.430
So I think, let me just pause for a moment

00:14:05.430 --> 00:14:06.840
and emphasize here.

00:14:06.840 --> 00:14:09.870
So the point is I think these aspects

00:14:09.870 --> 00:14:13.170
of the interventionist
program are actually good ways

00:14:13.170 --> 00:14:15.240
of characterizing independence postulates

00:14:15.240 --> 00:14:18.570
that play an essential
role in physics as well.

00:14:18.570 --> 00:14:21.600
And so I see these as
sort of a generalization

00:14:21.600 --> 00:14:24.060
from plausible physical principles

00:14:24.060 --> 00:14:26.220
that seem to play an important role

00:14:26.220 --> 00:14:28.530
in a variety of aspects of physics.

00:14:28.530 --> 00:14:31.410
Now I think, a natural
response that you might've had

00:14:31.410 --> 00:14:32.243
when I said I was going

00:14:32.243 --> 00:14:34.650
to use an interventionist
approach to causation

00:14:34.650 --> 00:14:38.400
and thinking about the
history of the deep past,

00:14:38.400 --> 00:14:40.590
is to say, "Interventions?

00:14:40.590 --> 00:14:42.240
Why are you talking about interventions?"

00:14:42.240 --> 00:14:43.073
Right?

00:14:44.262 --> 00:14:45.095
And this is just,

00:14:45.095 --> 00:14:47.853
again, we saw this
yesterday in several talks,

00:14:48.872 --> 00:14:50.520
a definition of cause

00:14:50.520 --> 00:14:53.970
from an interventionist program requires

00:14:53.970 --> 00:14:55.530
that it's in effect,

00:14:55.530 --> 00:14:58.983
you're able to pick out and
directly intervene on a cause

00:14:58.983 --> 00:15:02.790
that then has the intended effect.

00:15:02.790 --> 00:15:04.867
And so the question here would be,

00:15:04.867 --> 00:15:09.120
"Is it possible to control a variable Y

00:15:09.120 --> 00:15:12.210
and only that variable
by acting on its cause?"

00:15:12.210 --> 00:15:13.043
Right?

00:15:13.043 --> 00:15:16.950
So can you locate a sort
of surgical intervention

00:15:16.950 --> 00:15:18.840
in that kind of way?

00:15:18.840 --> 00:15:21.420
Now the reason why this
might seem implausible,

00:15:21.420 --> 00:15:25.500
is something we try to
apply to the deep past,

00:15:25.500 --> 00:15:27.930
is what could we mean
by possible here, right?

00:15:27.930 --> 00:15:30.600
So if you're doing an
experimental science,

00:15:30.600 --> 00:15:33.840
you could try to do these directly

00:15:33.840 --> 00:15:35.160
to assess the possibility.

00:15:35.160 --> 00:15:37.170
Because you could determine
whether your causal graph,

00:15:37.170 --> 00:15:39.900
is appropriately modeling
the data you're looking at

00:15:39.900 --> 00:15:42.903
by trying the interventions
and seeing what happens.

00:15:44.070 --> 00:15:45.630
But there's a basic question here

00:15:45.630 --> 00:15:48.360
about how do we assess possibility?

00:15:48.360 --> 00:15:49.440
And I think actually,

00:15:49.440 --> 00:15:51.780
so this goes back to Woodward's book

00:15:51.780 --> 00:15:54.000
from several years ago now,

00:15:54.000 --> 00:15:56.130
I don't think we need to think possible

00:15:56.130 --> 00:15:58.080
as just experimental manipulation.

00:15:58.080 --> 00:16:00.690
There's a much broader
notion of possibility here.

00:16:00.690 --> 00:16:03.120
And so as Woodward says,

00:16:03.120 --> 00:16:05.520
he doesn't even think it has
to mean physically possible.

00:16:05.520 --> 00:16:07.020
He has a little bit
further discussion here

00:16:07.020 --> 00:16:10.110
about a strong sense of
physical possibility and so on.

00:16:10.110 --> 00:16:13.740
But really the question is can
you define an intervention?

00:16:13.740 --> 00:16:15.740
Can you actually specify an intervention

00:16:16.830 --> 00:16:20.343
that would have the features
that we noted before?

00:16:21.247 --> 00:16:23.340
And I think in a lot of cases,

00:16:23.340 --> 00:16:26.610
we can actually think of
defining interventions

00:16:26.610 --> 00:16:28.080
in a much looser way,

00:16:28.080 --> 00:16:29.160
namely that we can think

00:16:29.160 --> 00:16:32.787
of obviously an experimental context,

00:16:32.787 --> 00:16:34.620
but we can think of natural experiments

00:16:34.620 --> 00:16:36.750
where we think of the
environment as having an impact

00:16:36.750 --> 00:16:37.800
on the system

00:16:37.800 --> 00:16:40.320
where it will influence
a particular variable.

00:16:40.320 --> 00:16:42.510
And so natural experiments
allow you to see

00:16:42.510 --> 00:16:45.570
that you can have, if nature cooperates,

00:16:45.570 --> 00:16:48.330
you can see the variation in that variable

00:16:48.330 --> 00:16:50.490
as a result of the way
the system interacts

00:16:50.490 --> 00:16:51.840
with the environment.

00:16:51.840 --> 00:16:55.980
So you have in effect
implemented that manipulation

00:16:55.980 --> 00:16:58.020
by looking at these separate systems.

00:16:58.020 --> 00:16:58.853
And I think,

00:16:58.853 --> 00:17:01.740
we also can assess these
questions of possibility

00:17:01.740 --> 00:17:03.450
in some cases by doing simulations

00:17:03.450 --> 00:17:05.670
where we can explore the possibility space

00:17:05.670 --> 00:17:06.930
and see what's possible

00:17:06.930 --> 00:17:10.830
in terms of varying, different variables.

00:17:10.830 --> 00:17:13.440
So it's not really something

00:17:13.440 --> 00:17:15.600
that is tied to human capabilities

00:17:15.600 --> 00:17:17.820
in this definition of causation.

00:17:17.820 --> 00:17:20.610
But I do think it's
really important to ask.

00:17:20.610 --> 00:17:23.370
So all of these assessments
do involve some kind

00:17:23.370 --> 00:17:24.750
of modal commitments.

00:17:24.750 --> 00:17:26.880
So what's the way that
the system interacts

00:17:26.880 --> 00:17:27.713
with the environment?

00:17:27.713 --> 00:17:29.430
How do we think about possibility?

00:17:29.430 --> 00:17:31.770
How's that enshrined in our simulations?

00:17:31.770 --> 00:17:33.210
So I think we do want to say

00:17:33.210 --> 00:17:34.830
that we can only use this

00:17:34.830 --> 00:17:36.000
if our modal assessments,

00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:38.760
at least have clear truth conditions,

00:17:38.760 --> 00:17:40.560
so that we can determine

00:17:40.560 --> 00:17:43.770
whether a particular
intervention is possible.

00:17:43.770 --> 00:17:45.420
Now let me say,

00:17:45.420 --> 00:17:48.870
if we think of our
anti-thermodynamics evolution

00:17:48.870 --> 00:17:53.870
and we imagine the gas now
evolving away from equilibrium

00:17:54.150 --> 00:17:56.550
and let's say we tried to
intervene on that system

00:17:56.550 --> 00:17:58.080
in some way, right,

00:17:58.080 --> 00:18:00.783
so that we would modify
its course of evolution,

00:18:01.718 --> 00:18:03.990
here, I think when we
start to think about this,

00:18:03.990 --> 00:18:05.940
we can see how hard it is to imagine

00:18:05.940 --> 00:18:08.440
what an intervention would
look like in that case.

00:18:09.450 --> 00:18:11.640
That's because we would,

00:18:11.640 --> 00:18:13.380
anything we might try to do,

00:18:13.380 --> 00:18:17.760
would disrupt the carefully
coordinated evolution

00:18:17.760 --> 00:18:20.070
that's leading it away from equilibrium.

00:18:20.070 --> 00:18:24.060
And so it seems like any
intervention is going to,

00:18:24.060 --> 00:18:25.470
rather than changing,

00:18:25.470 --> 00:18:29.310
let's say we want to change
something about the final state,

00:18:29.310 --> 00:18:31.230
any intervention is going
to change everything

00:18:31.230 --> 00:18:32.400
about the system, right?

00:18:32.400 --> 00:18:35.370
And so this is a point made by Adam Elga

00:18:35.370 --> 00:18:37.740
and then Porter Williams has
another recent discussion

00:18:37.740 --> 00:18:38.573
of this.

00:18:38.573 --> 00:18:41.160
So it's hard to see how you
could intervene in a system

00:18:41.160 --> 00:18:44.970
that had that kind of evolution.

00:18:44.970 --> 00:18:46.650
So that's a case where I'd say

00:18:46.650 --> 00:18:49.620
if you're trying to intervene in a world

00:18:49.620 --> 00:18:51.990
that had a failure of independence,

00:18:51.990 --> 00:18:54.330
then it seems I have no understanding

00:18:54.330 --> 00:18:57.120
of what interventions
would mean in that context.

00:18:57.120 --> 00:18:58.800
But I think in other contexts,

00:18:58.800 --> 00:19:01.470
we can say that we have an understanding

00:19:01.470 --> 00:19:03.870
of the modal assessment necessary

00:19:03.870 --> 00:19:06.300
to make these sorts of assessments.

00:19:06.300 --> 00:19:08.730
So I think we can, for example,

00:19:08.730 --> 00:19:10.290
going to the second point here,

00:19:10.290 --> 00:19:11.640
we can give a modal assessment

00:19:11.640 --> 00:19:13.650
of system-environment interactions,

00:19:13.650 --> 00:19:15.270
sources of exogenous variation,

00:19:15.270 --> 00:19:16.380
I think that's what people

00:19:16.380 --> 00:19:18.210
in historical sciences are often doing

00:19:18.210 --> 00:19:19.893
and have clear grounds for doing.

00:19:21.540 --> 00:19:23.220
As we'll come back to right at the end,

00:19:23.220 --> 00:19:25.560
I think it does get more tricky

00:19:25.560 --> 00:19:28.620
if we're talking about
global properties of systems,

00:19:28.620 --> 00:19:31.233
or global states or closed systems.

00:19:32.100 --> 00:19:35.760
And so the other thing, as
I just emphasized before,

00:19:35.760 --> 00:19:40.760
I think the interventionist
assumption of independence,

00:19:41.940 --> 00:19:43.680
the feature that I think
is really interesting,

00:19:43.680 --> 00:19:45.510
is that it depends not just on the laws,

00:19:45.510 --> 00:19:47.790
but it also depends on
features of the initial state.

00:19:47.790 --> 00:19:51.813
The initial state is uncorrelated
in an appropriate sense.

00:19:52.980 --> 00:19:54.510
So that's a brief summary

00:19:54.510 --> 00:19:58.080
of why I think the
interventionist approach,

00:19:58.080 --> 00:20:01.410
brings out some features
of causal inference

00:20:01.410 --> 00:20:04.890
that we can hopefully apply
to historical sciences.

00:20:04.890 --> 00:20:06.090
And I want to now shift

00:20:06.090 --> 00:20:09.990
to really briefly discuss
decoupling and traces.

00:20:09.990 --> 00:20:13.800
So inferring causes from traces,

00:20:13.800 --> 00:20:15.540
this is the standard kind of picture

00:20:15.540 --> 00:20:18.510
that Thomas mentioned before as well,

00:20:18.510 --> 00:20:20.790
that we see all kinds of cases

00:20:20.790 --> 00:20:22.230
where we say there's an event,

00:20:22.230 --> 00:20:24.510
such as a baseball going
through the window,

00:20:24.510 --> 00:20:26.490
you have a lot of evidence,

00:20:26.490 --> 00:20:28.980
the glass shards on the
floor, the baseball,

00:20:28.980 --> 00:20:30.360
your child's cheapish look,

00:20:30.360 --> 00:20:31.920
or if it was one of my daughters,

00:20:31.920 --> 00:20:33.495
you'd just see them
running out of the door

00:20:33.495 --> 00:20:34.328
(Christopher Smeenk giggles)

00:20:34.328 --> 00:20:35.670
to avoid what was coming next.

00:20:35.670 --> 00:20:39.630
But the traces then
overdetermine the cause.

00:20:39.630 --> 00:20:42.360
That's the sort of line
that Carol Cleland gives

00:20:42.360 --> 00:20:44.527
and I think there's something
that's right about it.

00:20:44.527 --> 00:20:45.360
(audience member coughs)

00:20:45.360 --> 00:20:46.193
But I think there's something

00:20:46.193 --> 00:20:48.090
that is missing in these accounts,

00:20:48.090 --> 00:20:50.430
which I think is actually really essential

00:20:50.430 --> 00:20:54.480
for, at least, the historical
sciences that I've looked at,

00:20:54.480 --> 00:20:56.040
which is that I think there are cases

00:20:56.040 --> 00:20:57.690
that are quite a bit more powerful

00:20:57.690 --> 00:20:59.550
than just inference from traces.

00:20:59.550 --> 00:21:02.790
So Dave gave us a beautiful
discussion of the CMB yesterday.

00:21:02.790 --> 00:21:04.584
So I deleted a bunch of slides.

00:21:04.584 --> 00:21:05.417
(Christopher Smeenk laughs)

00:21:05.417 --> 00:21:08.790
And the really crucial point
that I want to emphasize,

00:21:08.790 --> 00:21:13.110
is that after about 380,000
years after the Big Bang,

00:21:13.110 --> 00:21:15.000
the photons decouple

00:21:15.000 --> 00:21:20.000
and they free-stream
essentially to our satellites

00:21:20.640 --> 00:21:24.060
that are observing them or
our TVs if you have an old TV.

00:21:24.060 --> 00:21:26.403
And through that evolution,

00:21:28.751 --> 00:21:30.300
the photons themselves,

00:21:30.300 --> 00:21:33.090
the average properties for the whole sky,

00:21:33.090 --> 00:21:36.930
there's really no dynamical
process that is feasible

00:21:36.930 --> 00:21:40.320
that would modify the average
temperature of the CMB,

00:21:40.320 --> 00:21:43.800
other than the fact that due
to cosmological expansion,

00:21:43.800 --> 00:21:46.920
the temperature is changing
with the scale factor.

00:21:46.920 --> 00:21:49.920
So if you ask, "What is the mapping

00:21:49.920 --> 00:21:52.050
from the current
observations of the CMB back

00:21:52.050 --> 00:21:53.370
to the early universe?"

00:21:53.370 --> 00:21:54.570
It's really a direct mapping.

00:21:54.570 --> 00:21:58.020
So there are local variations,
which are quite important.

00:21:58.020 --> 00:22:00.870
But you have a really direct mapping back

00:22:00.870 --> 00:22:03.210
to the early state of the universe.

00:22:03.210 --> 00:22:05.730
And so I think this seems
to me stronger than a trace.

00:22:05.730 --> 00:22:09.420
And it's actually one thing
that Dave also emphasized,

00:22:09.420 --> 00:22:13.080
but I think is worth saying once more,

00:22:13.080 --> 00:22:17.520
the CMB has driven cosmology
for the last 50 years.

00:22:17.520 --> 00:22:20.400
So it's enabled precision
cosmology in a way

00:22:20.400 --> 00:22:22.950
that other observations
are now catching up to.

00:22:22.950 --> 00:22:25.410
But the level of precision

00:22:25.410 --> 00:22:27.720
that's available in
observations of the CMB

00:22:27.720 --> 00:22:30.300
and the fact that we've
kept coming back for more,

00:22:30.300 --> 00:22:32.130
there's more and more features of the CMB

00:22:32.130 --> 00:22:33.810
that have been revealing further details

00:22:33.810 --> 00:22:34.890
about the early universe

00:22:34.890 --> 00:22:37.020
that are very rich and important

00:22:37.020 --> 00:22:41.040
and constraining and understanding
cosmological theories.

00:22:41.040 --> 00:22:44.010
So I think it's a bit
stronger than just a trace.

00:22:44.010 --> 00:22:49.010
And so this is a group in 1957 at MIT,

00:22:49.050 --> 00:22:52.020
who are confident that this time capsule

00:22:52.020 --> 00:22:52.860
that they're installing,

00:22:52.860 --> 00:22:54.930
I don't know where it is on campus,

00:22:54.930 --> 00:22:57.030
would be dug up in 2957.

00:22:57.030 --> 00:22:59.040
So they expect it to last for a millennia.

00:22:59.040 --> 00:23:01.683
So that's a good, optimistic take.

00:23:02.580 --> 00:23:04.410
And I think that a lot of systems

00:23:04.410 --> 00:23:07.170
that people use in historical
sciences are time capsules

00:23:07.170 --> 00:23:10.380
in roughly the sense of this
sort of everyday time capsule.

00:23:10.380 --> 00:23:12.870
Namely it's a quasi-isolated system

00:23:12.870 --> 00:23:15.630
that evolves according to its
own dynamics in some sense,

00:23:15.630 --> 00:23:16.800
like the CMB,

00:23:16.800 --> 00:23:19.800
there's an autonomous dynamics
that governs the temperature.

00:23:21.210 --> 00:23:24.330
And it's quasi-isolated

00:23:24.330 --> 00:23:26.430
in the sense that there are
very few dynamical interactions

00:23:26.430 --> 00:23:30.150
that will disturb the
state of the time capsule.

00:23:30.150 --> 00:23:31.710
Obviously, this is defeasible,

00:23:31.710 --> 00:23:33.660
like it's something that
you can have failures

00:23:33.660 --> 00:23:35.910
of that sort of isolation.

00:23:35.910 --> 00:23:36.960
But there are a lot of cases

00:23:36.960 --> 00:23:40.140
where the time capsules also
give you further information,

00:23:40.140 --> 00:23:41.880
such as a internal clock

00:23:41.880 --> 00:23:44.640
that allows you to not just say infer

00:23:44.640 --> 00:23:46.440
what the state of a system was earlier,

00:23:46.440 --> 00:23:49.410
but infer something
about the time elapsed,

00:23:49.410 --> 00:23:51.750
since it decoupled from the environment.

00:23:51.750 --> 00:23:55.140
And so this is actually a really
rich source of information

00:23:55.140 --> 00:23:56.070
and I have one example

00:23:56.070 --> 00:23:59.610
that is something that you
might not have heard of before.

00:23:59.610 --> 00:24:02.520
So isotopic ratios are
a good time capsule,

00:24:02.520 --> 00:24:03.870
because there's very few things

00:24:03.870 --> 00:24:08.010
that change the isotopic ratios
for radioactive elements.

00:24:08.010 --> 00:24:11.220
And so this is a mine in Gabon

00:24:11.220 --> 00:24:14.130
that in the early seventies,

00:24:14.130 --> 00:24:16.590
people discovered something quite unusual

00:24:16.590 --> 00:24:18.540
and this was the Cold War era.

00:24:18.540 --> 00:24:23.540
The amount of uranium-235
compared to uranium-238 was wrong.

00:24:24.570 --> 00:24:26.970
And so they thought maybe there was some,

00:24:26.970 --> 00:24:28.560
maybe the Soviets had
been doing something,

00:24:28.560 --> 00:24:32.517
maybe there was some kind of
nuclear activity taking place.

00:24:32.517 --> 00:24:34.770
And it turns out there
was nuclear activity,

00:24:34.770 --> 00:24:39.300
but it was actually naturally
occurring nuclear reactors.

00:24:39.300 --> 00:24:44.300
So because of the low
uranium-235 abundances,

00:24:44.940 --> 00:24:46.350
they did some reconstructions.

00:24:46.350 --> 00:24:48.210
And so this is a map that shows,

00:24:48.210 --> 00:24:49.440
and the black spots,

00:24:49.440 --> 00:24:54.360
are where naturally occurring
fission reactors took place

00:24:54.360 --> 00:24:56.397
about 1.7 billion years ago.

00:24:56.397 --> 00:24:59.820
And so what this illustrates
is that there's a time capsule

00:24:59.820 --> 00:25:03.330
that the isotopic ratios are so secure

00:25:03.330 --> 00:25:04.590
that you're able to discover,

00:25:04.590 --> 00:25:08.940
like what could possibly have
changed those isotopic ratios?

00:25:08.940 --> 00:25:11.550
It's essentially only a fission reaction.

00:25:11.550 --> 00:25:12.990
And then they're able to reconstruct

00:25:12.990 --> 00:25:14.630
how could a fission
reaction have taken place.

00:25:14.630 --> 00:25:17.100
It has to do with the groundwater.

00:25:17.100 --> 00:25:18.510
1.7 billion years ago,

00:25:18.510 --> 00:25:21.870
there was more U-235 present

00:25:21.870 --> 00:25:22.920
and then they could confirm that

00:25:22.920 --> 00:25:24.630
by looking at other kind of decay products

00:25:24.630 --> 00:25:25.800
of the interaction.

00:25:25.800 --> 00:25:28.020
And they could see also
from the shape of the rock

00:25:28.020 --> 00:25:30.810
that it had been heated
up quite significantly.

00:25:30.810 --> 00:25:34.890
So that's just an example
of how the departure

00:25:34.890 --> 00:25:36.860
from this quasi-isolate dynamics,

00:25:36.860 --> 00:25:38.553
is also incredibly revealing.

00:25:39.450 --> 00:25:40.920
Now I've used examples from physics,

00:25:40.920 --> 00:25:42.510
just because that's what I know.

00:25:42.510 --> 00:25:45.720
But I would expect that there
are similar kinds of systems

00:25:45.720 --> 00:25:48.120
in biology and evolutionary biology

00:25:48.120 --> 00:25:49.530
where you can think of them

00:25:49.530 --> 00:25:50.970
as something more like a time capsule

00:25:50.970 --> 00:25:54.360
that gives you a very secure
way of inferring earlier states

00:25:54.360 --> 00:25:56.223
of the system of interest.

00:25:57.630 --> 00:26:00.420
Okay, so what does the
world have to be like

00:26:00.420 --> 00:26:02.400
to enable reconstructions
of the deep past?

00:26:02.400 --> 00:26:05.070
Well, I focus first on
worldly infrastructure.

00:26:05.070 --> 00:26:07.710
Now in terms of traces and time capsules,

00:26:07.710 --> 00:26:10.200
really, it's very similar to a theme

00:26:10.200 --> 00:26:12.390
that has come up in a
few of the talks before

00:26:12.390 --> 00:26:14.520
that what is important here,

00:26:14.520 --> 00:26:17.160
is a kind of dynamical
decoupling of systems

00:26:17.160 --> 00:26:18.120
from their environment,

00:26:18.120 --> 00:26:20.760
so that they evolve independently

00:26:20.760 --> 00:26:23.160
and they can be treated
as isolated systems.

00:26:23.160 --> 00:26:25.470
And that's something we've seen
in other contexts in physics

00:26:25.470 --> 00:26:27.450
where people have been using
effective field theories,

00:26:27.450 --> 00:26:28.283
very well

00:26:28.283 --> 00:26:29.460
where you have a kind of scale separation

00:26:29.460 --> 00:26:33.330
that allows you to treat
these as separable.

00:26:33.330 --> 00:26:35.340
Now obviously this is going to be,

00:26:35.340 --> 00:26:38.250
I don't know that I can say
much more generally about this,

00:26:38.250 --> 00:26:40.320
it's gonna be very
background-theory-dependent,

00:26:40.320 --> 00:26:41.790
being able to find a time capsule,

00:26:41.790 --> 00:26:43.320
figuring out how to use it.

00:26:43.320 --> 00:26:45.180
But I think our world does provide us

00:26:45.180 --> 00:26:46.650
with some of these time capsules

00:26:46.650 --> 00:26:49.353
and very persistent and revealing traces.

00:26:50.940 --> 00:26:55.350
Okay, so the very last brief comment

00:26:55.350 --> 00:26:56.970
on early universe cosmology.

00:26:56.970 --> 00:26:58.650
So I mentioned before,

00:26:58.650 --> 00:27:02.070
I think that there are some
issues regarding cosmology.

00:27:02.070 --> 00:27:03.270
But I first wanna say,

00:27:03.270 --> 00:27:06.150
I think this is a very
limited part of cosmology

00:27:06.150 --> 00:27:07.920
that poses particular problems.

00:27:07.920 --> 00:27:11.073
So I think in a lot of cases in cosmology,

00:27:11.940 --> 00:27:15.150
we actually can try to
make the modal assessment

00:27:15.150 --> 00:27:18.180
of whether something is
a possible intervention

00:27:18.180 --> 00:27:20.370
by the same ways that
we do in other contexts,

00:27:20.370 --> 00:27:22.200
so either through natural experiments,

00:27:22.200 --> 00:27:24.900
looking at a large
sample of similar systems

00:27:24.900 --> 00:27:27.090
and seeing how some features vary

00:27:27.090 --> 00:27:30.540
across the different
elements of that ensemble,

00:27:30.540 --> 00:27:33.240
or exploring possibility
spaces through simulations

00:27:33.240 --> 00:27:35.730
where we have a lot large
confidence in the theories

00:27:35.730 --> 00:27:38.190
that we're using to
construct those simulations.

00:27:38.190 --> 00:27:40.140
So I think for a lot of
questions in cosmology,

00:27:40.140 --> 00:27:42.750
we can answer these
interventionist questions,

00:27:42.750 --> 00:27:44.190
perfectly well.

00:27:44.190 --> 00:27:46.020
I think in some cases though,

00:27:46.020 --> 00:27:48.150
and in particular in the early universe,

00:27:48.150 --> 00:27:50.220
this is more of an n-equals-one problem

00:27:50.220 --> 00:27:52.410
as Dave emphasized last time.

00:27:52.410 --> 00:27:54.990
Because we don't have
any other realizations

00:27:54.990 --> 00:27:58.560
and then we face the difficulty
of drawing a contrast

00:27:58.560 --> 00:28:01.650
between law-like and contingent features.

00:28:01.650 --> 00:28:03.720
And furthermore, the
theory that's developed

00:28:03.720 --> 00:28:06.930
to render an observed history plausible

00:28:06.930 --> 00:28:07.800
to make it the case

00:28:07.800 --> 00:28:10.200
that what we see is to be expected,

00:28:10.200 --> 00:28:12.390
given our understanding of the dynamics,

00:28:12.390 --> 00:28:16.590
is also really only tested
through those same observations

00:28:16.590 --> 00:28:18.930
and the ability to reconstruct history.

00:28:18.930 --> 00:28:21.060
So there's a kind of worry
about a circularity there

00:28:21.060 --> 00:28:23.670
that I think is really hard to get around.

00:28:23.670 --> 00:28:25.320
So I think in that case,

00:28:25.320 --> 00:28:28.530
it's not clear that the
interventionist approach helps

00:28:28.530 --> 00:28:29.550
in addressing that problem,

00:28:29.550 --> 00:28:31.380
just because we face the problem

00:28:31.380 --> 00:28:34.440
of not really understanding modalities

00:28:34.440 --> 00:28:36.723
as clearly as we do in other contexts.

00:28:37.620 --> 00:28:40.027
Okay, so just to summarize then,

00:28:40.027 --> 00:28:41.190
"What does the world have to be like

00:28:41.190 --> 00:28:43.740
to enable reliable reconstructions?"

00:28:43.740 --> 00:28:46.110
So I've argued that the
kinds of assumptions

00:28:46.110 --> 00:28:47.910
that are needed to be in place

00:28:47.910 --> 00:28:52.260
for an interventionist causal analysis,

00:28:52.260 --> 00:28:54.030
also have to be in place here,

00:28:54.030 --> 00:28:56.130
that we need independence assumptions

00:28:56.130 --> 00:28:58.320
and then also to
understand the modal status

00:28:58.320 --> 00:28:59.910
of interventions.

00:28:59.910 --> 00:29:04.500
And also that I think there's
more interesting kinds

00:29:04.500 --> 00:29:05.333
of traces,

00:29:05.333 --> 00:29:06.990
which I've called time capsules

00:29:06.990 --> 00:29:09.570
that really do provide
a lot of information.

00:29:09.570 --> 00:29:11.190
And what seems to be the general feature

00:29:11.190 --> 00:29:12.750
that allows for those to exist,

00:29:12.750 --> 00:29:14.610
is a decoupling of systems

00:29:14.610 --> 00:29:18.090
that makes it possible
for those to be treated

00:29:18.090 --> 00:29:19.800
as effectively isolated.

00:29:19.800 --> 00:29:21.336
So thank you very much.

00:29:21.336 --> 00:29:24.503
(audience applauding)

00:29:27.136 --> 00:29:29.265
- [Moderator 1] Thank you much
Chris for keeping with time.

00:29:29.265 --> 00:29:30.265
- Questions?

00:29:32.226 --> 00:29:33.059
Eduardo.

00:29:34.170 --> 00:29:37.794
- [Eduardo] Thank you, Chris,
very, very interesting talk.

00:29:37.794 --> 00:29:41.250
I just wanna hear your thoughts.

00:29:41.250 --> 00:29:42.660
You were telling us

00:29:42.660 --> 00:29:47.100
how you feel comfortable
moving some data in cosmology

00:29:47.100 --> 00:29:48.600
from maybe traces to something

00:29:48.600 --> 00:29:50.820
that you would call
evidence or observation.

00:29:50.820 --> 00:29:54.510
And I wonder why do we need,

00:29:54.510 --> 00:29:55.960
I mean, I was just pulling up

00:29:55.960 --> 00:29:57.840
and I say that most of you probably know,

00:29:57.840 --> 00:30:00.693
because it's by Carl Sagan
on "The Burden of Skepticism"

00:30:00.693 --> 00:30:02.850
that I give to the students
all the time, right?

00:30:02.850 --> 00:30:03.683
- Mm-hmm (affirmative).

00:30:03.683 --> 00:30:05.310
- [Eduardo] Why do we need to do that,

00:30:05.310 --> 00:30:07.440
unless what we're trying to do is to move

00:30:07.440 --> 00:30:11.100
from just taking the result's face value

00:30:11.100 --> 00:30:14.190
to making decisions that are contingent

00:30:14.190 --> 00:30:17.010
on whether we're gonna
consider it a trace,

00:30:17.010 --> 00:30:18.630
or a confirm fact?

00:30:18.630 --> 00:30:19.463
And he was thinking,

00:30:19.463 --> 00:30:20.296
it (indistinct) made me think

00:30:20.296 --> 00:30:24.540
about how I would like
science or society to operate

00:30:24.540 --> 00:30:26.640
with the same kind of data

00:30:26.640 --> 00:30:30.750
if you're discussing father
and certainty in our monkeys-

00:30:30.750 --> 00:30:31.583
- Mm-hmm (affirmative).

00:30:31.583 --> 00:30:34.260
- [Eduardo] Or you have a
human being in a court of law,

00:30:34.260 --> 00:30:38.040
who may go to jail for my
assessments of paternity?

00:30:38.040 --> 00:30:41.190
It could be the same
numbers, the same data,

00:30:41.190 --> 00:30:45.090
but we may still decide
to call one not enough

00:30:45.090 --> 00:30:47.160
to make onto moving the decision.

00:30:47.160 --> 00:30:47.993
- Hmm.

00:30:47.993 --> 00:30:49.740
- [Eduardo] And the other
one I can live with making,

00:30:49.740 --> 00:30:52.350
I mean, I allow myself to call this a fact

00:30:52.350 --> 00:30:55.680
if I'm discussing all monkeys
in a workshop at Yale.

00:30:55.680 --> 00:30:56.513
- Hmm.

00:30:56.513 --> 00:30:57.346
- [Eduardo] But I may decide,

00:30:57.346 --> 00:30:59.010
"I'm gonna keep calling it a trace.

00:30:59.010 --> 00:31:02.280
Because I do not want to
pass judgment on somebody,

00:31:02.280 --> 00:31:05.310
who may or may not be the
father of this young."

00:31:05.310 --> 00:31:07.890
So I think again, it takes
us going back and forth

00:31:07.890 --> 00:31:10.830
between the actual results.

00:31:10.830 --> 00:31:11.864
- Hmm.

00:31:11.864 --> 00:31:13.200
- [Eduardo] And our assumption

00:31:13.200 --> 00:31:15.930
that we're gonna use
that for something else.

00:31:15.930 --> 00:31:18.420
- Right, no, I think
this is a general feature

00:31:18.420 --> 00:31:20.250
that epistemic standards

00:31:20.250 --> 00:31:23.253
that are appropriate to
different contexts shift, right?

00:31:25.823 --> 00:31:27.420
And I think cosmology,

00:31:27.420 --> 00:31:29.970
there aren't as many decision contexts

00:31:29.970 --> 00:31:32.160
where it really matters

00:31:32.160 --> 00:31:34.470
if you've got the right value
of the Hubble constant, right?

00:31:34.470 --> 00:31:38.370
So I think there's different ways

00:31:38.370 --> 00:31:41.400
of asking the decision question though,

00:31:41.400 --> 00:31:44.640
which I think, if you're
going to take something

00:31:44.640 --> 00:31:47.370
as giving you insight
into the causal structure

00:31:47.370 --> 00:31:48.843
of how the universe evolved,

00:31:49.680 --> 00:31:51.450
I think that gives you a much richer sense

00:31:51.450 --> 00:31:53.400
of the further consequences of that.

00:31:53.400 --> 00:31:54.900
And so some of,

00:31:54.900 --> 00:31:56.460
this is a slightly different question,

00:31:56.460 --> 00:31:57.293
you're asking more,

00:31:57.293 --> 00:32:00.090
like "How should we classify
something as a trace,

00:32:00.090 --> 00:32:01.380
or how confident should we be

00:32:01.380 --> 00:32:03.230
in thinking of it as a time capsule?"

00:32:04.200 --> 00:32:06.270
There's also then if you
use it in a certain way,

00:32:06.270 --> 00:32:08.010
you might get a better picture

00:32:08.010 --> 00:32:09.960
of like here's the causal structure.

00:32:09.960 --> 00:32:11.700
And then that might have
further implications

00:32:11.700 --> 00:32:14.160
for other aspects of
cosmological modeling, right?

00:32:14.160 --> 00:32:16.260
So I think in cosmology,

00:32:16.260 --> 00:32:20.370
just because it's intrinsically,
incredibly valuable field,

00:32:20.370 --> 00:32:23.730
but not something that has
like direct extrinsic impact

00:32:23.730 --> 00:32:25.710
on day-to-day decisions for the most part,

00:32:25.710 --> 00:32:27.270
I think that's gonna be the impact.

00:32:27.270 --> 00:32:29.460
It's more on the implications
for other aspects

00:32:29.460 --> 00:32:30.813
of cosmological modeling.

00:32:31.800 --> 00:32:34.470
And then yeah, I think your
degree of confidence can shift.

00:32:34.470 --> 00:32:35.303
Like if it's something

00:32:35.303 --> 00:32:39.690
that you think is an essential
feature of your modeling

00:32:39.690 --> 00:32:40.770
and you're gonna build

00:32:40.770 --> 00:32:43.320
like 20 years worth of research on this,

00:32:43.320 --> 00:32:44.400
you'd probably have a,

00:32:44.400 --> 00:32:46.860
or I think a different way this comes up,

00:32:46.860 --> 00:32:50.670
is like motivating large-scale,
very expensive telescopes.

00:32:50.670 --> 00:32:52.200
You wanna have a lot of confidence

00:32:52.200 --> 00:32:55.020
that what you're looking
for is really something

00:32:55.020 --> 00:32:57.810
that's going to be very decisive

00:32:57.810 --> 00:32:59.640
in answering the question
you're interested in.

00:32:59.640 --> 00:33:02.310
And so you might then have
a very careful analysis

00:33:02.310 --> 00:33:05.832
of like how certain are we
about this as a source of-

00:33:05.832 --> 00:33:06.960
- It's when we want to make decisions

00:33:06.960 --> 00:33:08.700
about public money investment,

00:33:08.700 --> 00:33:09.930
right, for example.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:33:09.930 --> 00:33:11.043
- Yeah, Alex?

00:33:13.800 --> 00:33:14.633
- Thanks.

00:33:14.633 --> 00:33:16.740
I wanted to just ask a bit more

00:33:16.740 --> 00:33:20.739
about the focus on the deep
past as opposed to recent past.

00:33:20.739 --> 00:33:21.572
- [Christopher Smeenk] Right.

00:33:21.572 --> 00:33:23.760
- Because it seemed like this question

00:33:23.760 --> 00:33:25.260
of kind of what's the scope and limits

00:33:25.260 --> 00:33:26.460
of our knowledge about the deep past?

00:33:26.460 --> 00:33:28.290
You could also ask it
about the recent past

00:33:28.290 --> 00:33:32.490
and you were saying that
current or recent things,

00:33:32.490 --> 00:33:33.930
we can like perform experiments

00:33:33.930 --> 00:33:35.970
in a way that we can't
directly perform experiments

00:33:35.970 --> 00:33:37.320
about the deep past.

00:33:37.320 --> 00:33:38.490
But on the other hand,

00:33:38.490 --> 00:33:39.480
it is still true

00:33:39.480 --> 00:33:42.030
that in order to be able
to trust our memories

00:33:42.030 --> 00:33:43.410
of the recent past,

00:33:43.410 --> 00:33:44.790
we're relying on assumptions

00:33:44.790 --> 00:33:46.380
about there aren't a lot

00:33:46.380 --> 00:33:49.170
of anti-thermodynamic things happening.

00:33:49.170 --> 00:33:51.630
So I was wondering like
the conditions you listed,

00:33:51.630 --> 00:33:54.720
would you also think them
to be basically necessary

00:33:54.720 --> 00:33:56.400
for knowledge of the recent past?

00:33:56.400 --> 00:34:00.033
And if so, then how do you see
the focus on the deep past?

00:34:01.050 --> 00:34:02.730
What's the importance of that focus,

00:34:02.730 --> 00:34:04.740
as opposed to just focus
on the past in general?

00:34:04.740 --> 00:34:06.150
- Yeah, no, so there's a lot of the story

00:34:06.150 --> 00:34:08.610
that I think would be roughly
the same for the recent past.

00:34:08.610 --> 00:34:10.530
So you have to have an account of memory,

00:34:10.530 --> 00:34:12.330
you have to have an account of how it is

00:34:12.330 --> 00:34:15.570
that you form records or take
certain things as records.

00:34:15.570 --> 00:34:17.370
And I think that doesn't
really discriminate

00:34:17.370 --> 00:34:19.560
between the deep past and recent past.

00:34:19.560 --> 00:34:22.650
So a lot of what I say, I
think just does carry over.

00:34:22.650 --> 00:34:25.950
But I think partly,
it's within the context

00:34:25.950 --> 00:34:29.010
of the recent discussions
of historical sciences

00:34:29.010 --> 00:34:32.160
that people are stating
these kinds of pessimism

00:34:32.160 --> 00:34:34.950
that do depend on the
amount of time-lapse.

00:34:34.950 --> 00:34:38.580
So even so I think for
some of the skeptics,

00:34:38.580 --> 00:34:39.750
even if you imagine

00:34:39.750 --> 00:34:43.080
that "If my daughter broke
the window yesterday,

00:34:43.080 --> 00:34:44.760
I could find the traces,"

00:34:44.760 --> 00:34:47.130
if that's the way you think
of the evidences working

00:34:47.130 --> 00:34:49.980
and you think that those
traces decay over time,

00:34:49.980 --> 00:34:51.750
then you have a reason for thinking

00:34:51.750 --> 00:34:53.160
that the account of the recent past,

00:34:53.160 --> 00:34:55.080
just won't extend that far.

00:34:55.080 --> 00:34:56.130
And part of what I'm arguing,

00:34:56.130 --> 00:34:59.160
is actually if you have
something like a time capsule,

00:34:59.160 --> 00:35:03.056
that actually can be stable
over 13.8 billion years, right?

00:35:03.056 --> 00:35:03.889
(Christopher Smeenk giggles)

00:35:03.889 --> 00:35:05.220
So I think that some of what I'm saying,

